<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Velu Thampi Dalawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a>–<a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Velu Thampi Dalawa was the chief minister, or dalawa, of the kingdom of Travancore from 1802 to 1809. After reforming its administration and finances, he clashed with the British Resident over the subsidy demanded under the treaty of 1805, and in 1809 he led an armed revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, announced in his Kundara Proclamation. When it failed he killed himself to avoid capture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/velu-thampi-dalawa.jpg" alt="Velu Thampi Dalawa" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kiran Gopi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veluthampi.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Velayudhan Chempakaraman Thampi was born on 6 May 1765 at Thalakkulam in southern Travancore, now in the Kanyakumari district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a> family; his parents were Manakkara Kunju Mayatti Pillai and Valiyamma Pillai Thankachi. His family held the high title of Chempakaraman for its services to the state created by Maharajah Marthanda Varma. Early in the reign of Maharajah Dharmaraja Ramavarma he was appointed Kariakkar, or tahsildar, at Mavelikkara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He rose under Bala Rama Varma, whose reign was marked by unrest. The dewan Raja Kesavadas had been deposed and found dead under house arrest, and his successor Jayanthan Sankaran Nampoothiri, whom the British Resident Colonel Macaulay accused of the murder, had emptied the treasury. The Madras Government confirmed Velu Thampi as Dalawa within a few months. His severity angered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a> nobility, and when the courtier Samprathi Kunjunilam Pillai obtained a royal warrant for his execution, Macaulay gave him British soldiers; Kunjunilam Pillai was found guilty of murder and conspiracy.</p><p>In 1804 his plan to cut the allowances of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a> troops set off a mutiny: 10,000 soldiers marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Trivandrum</a>, and on 16 April Nair soldiers at Alappuzha tried to kill him. At the head of the Carnatic Brigade he crushed the revolt and had many of its leaders executed. The Treaty of 1805 then enlarged the Company force in Travancore and set a subsidy of Rs. 800,000 a year. By the end of 1808 the payments were long in arrears, and Macaulay pressed for them in language Thampi found insulting.</p><p>Velu Thampi and the Paliath Achan, Govindan Menon, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> resolved to end British supremacy and planned to storm the Fort of Cochin and kill the Resident. Thampi appealed in vain to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> and the French. As British troops approached he fled to Kilimanoor, where he left his sword with the family that sheltered him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajah</a> turned against him, an armistice was signed on 26 February 1809, and a reward of 50,000 Travancore rupees was offered for his capture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His enemy Ummini Thampi had the family's ancestral house levelled, and most of his relatives were banished to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>. The sword stayed with the Kilimanoor royal family for about 150 years, was presented to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-prasad">Rajendra Prasad</a> in 1957 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> on 20 June 2010, to the Napier Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Thiruvananthapuram</a>. A commemorative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postage-stamp">postage stamp</a> was issued on 6 May 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Velu Thampi Dalawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thalakulam, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=8.31,77.53&amp;year=1793" aria-label="Thalakulam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6693 1853 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Velu Thampi Dalawa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7143 2123h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7143 2123h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=8.31,77.53&amp;year=1793">What was here in 1793? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
